        <div class="tab-content" id="tab-os" data-main="devices" data-sub="os">
            <div class="info">
                <strong>force OS updates</strong> runs on every non-excluded Windows and Linux device: on Windows, the built-in
                Windows Update agent searches, downloads and installs all pending updates (feature upgrades excluded). On Linux,
                the distribution's package manager runs (<code>apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get upgrade</code>, <code>dnf upgrade</code>,
                <code>yum update</code>, <code>zypper update</code>, <code>pacman -Syu</code> or <code>apk upgrade</code>).<br>
                <strong>force OS Upgrade</strong> only targets devices whose OS release is <em>provably not current</em> (current
                according to the endoflife.date data: <span id="osCurrentInfo">-</span>). There is no built-in fallback version: without
                fresh data (at most 30 days old) the release state is shown as <em>unknown</em> with the reason, and such devices are
                not upgraded. An outdated release is <strong>Upgrade urgent</strong> when its own support has ended or ends within
                <span id="osUrgentDaysInfo">-</span> days (Settings tab), otherwise <strong>Upgrade available</strong>; without a known
                support end it is never called urgent. <em>force OS Upgrade (urgent)</em> covers only the urgent devices,
                <em>force OS Upgrade (all)</em> every outdated device. On Windows 10/11, Windows Update is pointed at the current release with the
                documented TargetReleaseVersion policy and installs it; the previous policy is restored afterwards. On Ubuntu,
                <code>apt-get dist-upgrade</code> runs, followed by <code>do-release-upgrade</code>, which moves one release per run.
                Other distributions and Windows Server are shown but never upgraded automatically.<br>
                <strong>Reboot policy:</strong> these OS runs never reboot the device themselves - if the updates
                need a reboot, that is reported per device and Windows performs it at its own
                automatic-restart deadline / outside the active hours, or you reboot it yourself.<br>
                <strong>Windows Update troubleshooter (per Windows device):</strong> <em>Diagnose</em> reads the WU
                services, whether a reboot is pending (and when Windows will restart automatically), the download
                cache size and the last <code>WindowsUpdateClient</code> errors - it changes nothing.
                <em>Troubleshoot</em> applies the repairs the built-in troubleshooter would: it restarts
                BITS/wuauserv/appidsvc/cryptsvc/msiserver, resets <code>SoftwareDistribution\Download</code> and
                <code>catroot2</code> and re-asserts the Automatic start types. It does not reboot - any pending
                reboot is reported with when it will happen. The winget exclusion list applies here too, and an OS
                batch never runs at the same time as a winget batch or a troubleshooter run on that device.<br>
                <strong>Restart / Notify user (per Windows or Linux device, v4.7.0):</strong> <em>Notify user</em>
                shows a message to the logged-on user (Windows <code>msg</code>, Linux <code>wall</code>) without
                rebooting. <em>Restart</em> reboots the device on purpose after a short grace, sending that same
                message through the OS shutdown banner - this and the PC Reset tab are the ONLY two places this
                plugin ever restarts a machine, and both are explicit, per-device and need the device name typed
                (never automatic, never a batch, never scheduled). Use it to reboot before or after an upgrade.
            </div>
            <div class="controls">
