        <div class="tab-content" id="tab-reset" data-main="devices" data-sub="reset">
            <div class="info">
                <strong>This is the one feature that restarts a device.</strong> It resets a single
                <strong>Windows</strong> PC with the built-in <code>systemreset</code> tool. A reset cannot run from inside
                the booted OS: as soon as it starts, the machine <strong>reboots into Windows RE and resets itself</strong>
                there, so it disappears from the console and comes back as a fresh install. For that reason this is
                <strong>per-device, manual and never automatic</strong> - no fleet, no schedule - and you must
                <strong>type the device name</strong> to confirm before anything is sent.<br>
                Two modes: <strong>Keep my files</strong> removes apps and settings but keeps personal files;
                <strong>Remove everything</strong> wipes the drive. Press <em>Check</em> first: it reads, without changing
                anything, whether <code>systemreset</code> is present, the build supports the non-interactive switches
                (Windows 10 1809 / build <span id="drMinBuild">-</span> or newer), Windows RE is enabled and whether
                <strong>BitLocker</strong> protects the system drive. <strong>Remove everything is blocked while BitLocker
                protection is on</strong> (or its state is unknown) until you confirm the recovery key is available -
                otherwise the device comes back locked. A reset only runs on a device that is online, idle (no winget,
                OS, deploy or another reset session) and passed its check within the last 6 hours.
            </div>
